              "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat > .fractal-factory/audit-report.json << 'AUDIT_EOF'\\n{\\n  \\\"version\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"lastUpdated\\\": \\\"2026-03-16T09:12:00Z\\\",\\n  \\\"iteration\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"reEntryContext\\\": \\\"Re-entry from gap-hunting cycle 1 (22 gaps: 3 critical, 19 warnings). All gaps addressed. Checklist-validator passed 1,331/1,331. This audit provides independent architectural verification.\\\",\\n  \\\"overallVerdict\\\": \\\"issues-found\\\",\\n  \\\"perspectives\\\": {\\n    \\\"agentAsFunction\\\": {\\n      \\\"checksRun\\\": 15,\\n      \\\"checksPassing\\\": 10,\\n      \\\"checksFailing\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"findings\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-CONTRACT-02\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"skill:agent-as-function-contract\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Skill file result code table misattributes two result codes. It says 'revision-required' is used by 'Beta-reading-coordinator' — but revision-required is NOT a result code; it is a data value inside beta-synthesis/{N}.json artifacts. It says 'revision-loop' is used by 'Orchestrator' — but revision-loop is emitted by coordinators, not the orchestrator. The orchestrator ROUTES on revision-loop but does not EMIT it.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"In skills/agent-as-function-contract/SKILL.md, the result code table has: '| revision-required | Beta readers request another revision pass | Beta-reading-coordinator |' and '| revision-loop | Orchestrator routes back to revision from beta | Orchestrator |'. Beta-reading-coordinator's actual result codes per roster.json and prompt are: complete, blocked, revision-loop. No agent emits 'revision-required' as a status.json result. The orchestrator's result codes are: delivered, delivered-with-gaps, failed.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Fix the skill file table: revision-required should be labeled as a beta-synthesis data field (not a status result code), or removed from the table. revision-loop should be attributed to 'All coordinators (8)' with the Orchestrator listed as consumer, not producer.\\\",\\n          \\\"previousCycle\\\": \\\"new\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-MANIFEST-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"info\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"20 coordinators/sub-coordinators + orchestrator\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"21 agents (all 8 coordinators, all 10 sub-coordinators, the orchestrator, and the guide) do not reference manifest.json prepend instructions. The manifest schema says 'Every agent prepends an entry after completing work' but pure routers write no creative artifacts — only status.json routing signals.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"grep -ci 'manifest' across all 67 agent files shows 46 with manifest references, 21 without. The 21 without are all coordinators, sub-coordinators, the orchestrator, and the guide. None of these agents write creative artifacts.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Either: (a) amend the manifest schema to say 'Every specialist agent prepends' (excluding routers), or (b) add manifest prepend to all 21 agents for complete execution tracing. Option (a) is simpler and architecturally cleaner.\\\",\\n          \\\"previousCycle\\\": \\\"carried-over-from-cycle-0\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-ARTIFACT-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"craft-profile.schema.md\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Phantom agent reference in schema. craft-profile.schema.md says 'may be augmented during plotting by romantic-fantasy-writer-plot-architect' — but no agent named plot-architect exists in the roster or agent files. Only craft-profile-selector writes craft-profile.json per the roster. This is a stale reference that could confuse agent implementations.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"In schemas/craft-profile.schema.md: 'read-modify-write — Initially created by romantic-fantasy-writer-concept-developer, may be augmented during plotting by romantic-fantasy-writer-plot-architect.' and '- romantic-fantasy-writer-plot-architect (may add tools during plotting)'. But: ls agents/*plot-architect* returns empty. roster.json has no agent with 'plot-architect' in name. Only craft-profile-selector appears in roster writes for craft-profile.json.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Remove the plot-architect reference from craft-profile.schema.md. If plotting agents should be able to augment the craft profile, add craft-profile.json to the appropriate specialist's writes list and update the schema writers section.\\\",\\n          \\\"previousCycle\\\": \\\"new\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-ARTIFACT-02\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"chapters.schema.md, chapter-drafter, pov-voice-maintainer, continuity-integrator, craft-enforcer\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Schema declares chapters/{N}/draft.md as 'create-once' written only by chapter-drafter, but 3 additional agents (pov-voice-maintainer, continuity-integrator, craft-enforcer) are declared as writers in roster.json and their prompts explicitly describe editing the draft. The schema should declare a read-modify-write protocol with sequential ordering to match actual behavior.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"chapters.schema.md: '### Write Protocol\\\\\\\\n**create-once** — Written by romantic-fantasy-writer-chapter-drafter.' But roster.json lists 4 writers for chapters/{N}/draft.md: chapter-drafter, pov-voice-maintainer, continuity-integrator, craft-enforcer. pov-voice-maintainer.agent.md: 'Edit chapters/{N}/draft.md to correct voice inconsistencies.' continuity-integrator.agent.md: 'edit chapters/{N}/draft.md to resolve it.'\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Change chapters/{N}/draft.md write protocol from 'create-once' to 'read-modify-write' with explicit sequential ordering: (1) chapter-drafter creates, (2) pov-voice-maintainer edits voice, (3) continuity-integrator fixes continuity, (4) craft-enforcer enforces craft tools. Add ownership rules: each editor preserves prior editors' changes.\\\",\\n          \\\"previousCycle\\\": \\\"carried-over-from-cycle-0-with-new-detail\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"AF-ARTIFACT-04\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"pov-voice-maintainer, continuity-integrator, craft-enforcer\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Create-once artifact chapters/{N}/draft.md is written by 4 agents, violating the create-once contract. This is the implementation side of the AF-ARTIFACT-02 schema mismatch. The 3 editing agents (pov-voice-maintainer, continuity-integrator, craft-enforcer) modify a file declared as create-once.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"roster.json declares 4 writers for chapters/{N}/draft.md. chapters.schema.md declares 'create-once'. The Rules skill (rules/SKILL.md) says: 'create-once: Check that the file does NOT exist before writing. If it exists, you are in a retry.' — if the editors follow this literally, they would interpret the existing draft as a retry signal rather than the expected multi-pass editing workflow.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Resolve jointly with AF-ARTIFACT-02. Change schema to read-modify-write. Add a specific 'multi-pass-edit' protocol variant to the Rules skill that distinguishes between retry (auditor sent you back) and sequential editing (you're the next editor in the pipeline).\\\",\\n          \\\"previousCycle\\\": \\\"new-detail\\\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"fractalWorkflow\\\": {\\n      \\\"checksRun\\\": 19,\\n      \\\"checksPassing\\\": 14,\\n      \\\"checksFailing\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"findings\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"FW-HIERARCHY-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"info\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"romantic-fantasy-writer (orchestrator)\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Orchestrator dispatches 3 specialist-level agents directly: craft-tracker (between style and drafting), continuity-tracker (between drafting and revision), series-kb-manager (after polish). These are cross-cutting concerns that don't naturally belong under any phase coordinator.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Orchestrator routing table: 'dispatch romantic-fantasy-writer-craft-tracker (initialize)', 'dispatch romantic-fantasy-writer-continuity-tracker (full verification)', 'dispatch romantic-fantasy-writer-series-kb-manager'. roster.json confirms all three have level: 'specialist'.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Architecturally justified — wrapping each in a single-agent coordinator adds routing complexity without benefit. Document this as an intentional deviation: 'Cross-cutting specialists are dispatched directly by the orchestrator when they operate at pipeline transition boundaries rather than within a creative phase.'\\\",\\n          \\\"previousCycle\\\": \\\"carried-over-from-cycle-0\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"FW-PURITY-03\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"all 8 coordinators\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"revision-loop is declared as a result code for all 8 coordinators (concept, worldbuilding, character, plotting, style, drafting, revision, beta-reading) but no coordinator routing table rule ever emits it. The routing tables have explicit rules for complete, blocked, auditor-passed, auditor-failed — but no rule writes revision-loop. The fractal-coordinator-patterns skill describes the retry loop but doesn't specify when to write revision-loop as status.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"For all 8 coordinators: grep for 'write own.*revision-loop' or 'status.*revision-loop' returns 0 matches. Routing tables only write 'complete' or 'blocked'. Yet roster.json lists revision-loop in resultCodes for all 8. The orchestrator has a routing rule for it: 'agents/{any-coordinator}/status.json | result == revision-loop | Wait'. This is a dead routing path — the condition can never be triggered because no agent emits the result.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Either: (a) add an explicit routing table entry in each coordinator that emits revision-loop when starting an auditor retry loop (before deleting child statuses), giving the orchestrator a heartbeat signal, or (b) remove revision-loop from coordinator resultCodes and the orchestrator routing table if it is never needed. Option (a) is preferred for observability.\\\",\\n          \\\"previousCycle\\\": \\\"new\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"FW-LAZY-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"beta-synthesizer, romance-beta-reader, fantasy-beta-reader, craft-beta-reader, sensitivity-beta-reader, originality-beta-reader\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Six adversarial agents lack Anti-Laziness Rules sections entirely. The beta-synthesizer aggregates findings and assigns severity ratings that drive the revision-required decision — without anti-laziness rules, it could under-rate severity to avoid triggering revision cycles. The 5 beta readers are the adversarial review layer that must find real issues — without anti-laziness rules, they could produce shallow feedback that misses critical problems. All 8 phase auditors HAVE anti-laziness rules, but the beta-reading path's adversarial agents do not.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"grep -c 'Anti-Laziness' returns 0 for all 6 agents: beta-synthesizer.agent.md, romance-beta-reader.agent.md, fantasy-beta-reader.agent.md, craft-beta-reader.agent.md, sensitivity-beta-reader.agent.md, originality-beta-reader.agent.md. The beta-reading-auditor (their gate-keeper) HAS anti-laziness rules, but the content producers do not.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Add Anti-Laziness Rules sections to all 6 agents. For beta readers: 'If you find zero issues in a full chapter, that is suspicious — re-read focusing on [lens-specific weak spots]. Your feedback must cite specific passages with line references.' For beta-synthesizer: 'If you find zero conflicts between 5 independent lenses, that is suspicious. If you downgrade all critical findings to minor, justify each downgrade with evidence.'\\\",\\n          \\\"previousCycle\\\": \\\"carried-over-from-cycle-0\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"FW-LAZY-04\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"character-auditor, plotting-auditor, style-auditor, revision-auditor\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"4 of 8 phase auditors lack explicit zero-findings suspicion clauses in their Anti-Laziness Rules. Without this clause, an auditor could pass work with zero findings — which for a multi-artifact creative phase is statistically improbable. The worldbuilding-auditor, concept-auditor, drafting-auditor, and beta-reading-auditor DO have this clause.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"character-auditor Anti-Laziness Rules: 5 numbered items, none mention zero-findings suspicion. plotting-auditor: 5 items, none mention it. style-auditor: 5 items, none mention it. revision-auditor: has evidence requirement but no zero-findings clause. Compare with worldbuilding-auditor: 'If your first pass finds zero issues, that is suspicious — do a second pass.' and concept-auditor: includes zero-findings suspicion.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Add a zero-findings suspicion clause to all 4 auditors. Template: 'If your first pass finds zero issues, that is suspicious. Run a second pass focusing on [phase-specific common failure modes]. A zero-finding audit of a [phase-specific artifact count] artifact set is statistically improbable.'\\\",\\n          \\\"previousCycle\\\": \\\"new-detail\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"id\\\": \\\"FW-ADDITIONAL-01\\\",\\n          \\\"severity\\\": \\\"warning\\\",\\n          \\\"agent\\\": \\\"all specialist agents\\\",\\n          \\\"description\\\": \\\"Specialist agents have no retry awareness in their prompts. The retry mechanism relies entirely on skill file indirection: the Rules skill says create-once files existing means retry, and the coordinator-patterns skill says writers MUST read audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json on retry. But individual specialist prompts (a) don't list gate files in their Reads, (b) have no process step for checking existing output, and (c) have no instructions for incorporating auditor feedback. The concept-developer reads ONLY story-config.json per its prompt. The geography-builder has zero retry-related content.\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"concept-developer.agent.md Reads: 'story-config.json' — no gate file. concept-developer.agent.md Process: 8 linear steps with no retry check. grep for 'retry|gate|auditor feedback' in concept-developer returns 0. fractal-coordinator-patterns/SKILL.md says: 'Feedback artifact path: audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json — writers MUST read this on retry'. But this instruction lives only in the skill, not in the specialist prompts.\\\",\\n          \\\"recommendation\\\": \\\"Add a 'Step 0: Check for Retry' to every specialist prompt: 'If your primary output artifact already exists, you are in a retry. Read audit-reports/{your-phase}/gate.json for auditor feedback. Focus your improvements on the specific issues flagged.' Also add audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json to each specialist's Reads list in roster.json.\\\",\\n          \\\"previousCycle\\\": \\\"new\\\"\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"gapRemediationVerification\\\": {\\n    \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Verified remediation of the 3 critical and 19 warning gaps from cycle 1.\\\",\\n    \\\"criticalGaps\\\": {\\n      \\\"IE-001_INV030\\\": {\\n        \\\"status\\\": \\\"verified-fixed\\\",\\n        \\\"detail\\\": \\\"INV-030 (No Silent Failures) now referenced by all 67 agents. grep confirms 67/67 files contain INV-030. Each agent includes the invariant in Key Invariants section with appropriate language about surfacing problems in status.json.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"IE-002_thematic_argument\\\": {\\n        \\\"status\\\": \\\"verified-fixed-by-design\\\",\\n        \\\"detail\\\": \\\"INV-063/T25 (Thematic Argument Scaffolding) is handled through the dynamic craft-profile mechanism. craft-profile-selector selects craft tools, auditors load craft-profile.json and check all selected tools. The coverage is implicit through the craft-profile pattern rather than explicit enumeration in every auditor.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"general_critical_gaps\\\": {\\n        \\\"status\\\": \\\"verified-fixed\\\",\\n        \\\"detail\\\": \\\"All 67 agent prompt files exist, all match roster entries, all have correct parent/child relationships, all reference appropriate skills.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"warningGapsSpotCheck\\\": {\\n      \\\"checked\\\": 10,\\n      \\\"allFixed\\\": true,\\n      \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Spot-checked 10 of 19 warning gaps. All appear architecturally addressed. Coordinator purity rules confirmed for all 18 coordinators/sub-coordinators. Skills consistently referenced across all agents.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"comparisonWithChecklistValidator\\\": {\\n    \\\"checklistValidatorResult\\\": \\\"pass (1,331/1,331)\\\",\\n    \\\"newIssuesFoundByAudit\\\": 5,\\n    \\\"issuesSummary\\\": \\\"The checklist validator confirmed structural completeness. This audit found 5 architectural/semantic issues the structural checklist cannot detect: (1) skill file result-code table inaccuracies, (2) phantom agent reference in schema, (3) schema/implementation protocol mismatch for draft.md, (4) revision-loop dead routing path, (5) specialist retry feedback gap. These are architectural coherence issues requiring semantic understanding, not structural checks.\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"summary\\\": {\\n    \\\"totalChecks\\\": 34,\\n    \\\"totalPassing\\\": 24,\\n    \\\"totalFailing\\\": 10,\\n    \\\"criticalFindings\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"warningFindings\\\": 8,\\n    \\\"infoFindings\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"newIssues\\\": \\\"5 new architectural issues not caught by checklist-validator or previous audit: AF-CONTRACT-02 (skill table inaccuracy), AF-ARTIFACT-01 (phantom agent), FW-PURITY-03 (revision-loop dead path), FW-LAZY-04 (auditor zero-findings gaps), FW-ADDITIONAL-01 (specialist retry feedback gap)\\\",\\n    \\\"carriedOverIssues\\\": \\\"3 issues carried over from cycle 0 with updated analysis: AF-MANIFEST-01 (router manifest — info), FW-HIERARCHY-01 (orchestrator dispatches specialists — info), FW-LAZY-01 (beta reader anti-laziness — warning)\\\",\\n    \\\"architecturalHealth\\\": \\\"Good. The system has sound pipeline architecture, consistent hierarchy, proper convergence bounds, and comprehensive invariant coverage after the INV-030 fix. The remaining issues are in documentation consistency (schema/prompt alignment), adversarial enforcement completeness (beta reader anti-laziness), and retry mechanism clarity (specialist retry awareness). No issues threaten system correctness at a fundamental level — they affect quality assurance depth and documentation accuracy.\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\nAUDIT_EOF\\necho \\\"Audit report written successfully\\\"\", \"description\": \"Write audit report\", \"initial_wait\": 10, \"shellId\": \"writeAudit\"}"
