        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "id": "toolu_017vN1kmf5DnSGuG3P1nkvT7",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/agents/fractal-factory-audit-oracle/output.md\", \"file_text\": \"# Audit Oracle — Iteration 2 (Re-Entry Verification)\\n\\n**Agent:** fractal-factory-audit-oracle\\n**Iteration:** 2\\n**Context:** Re-entry from gap-hunting cycle 1 (22 gaps addressed)\\n**Checklist-Validator Status:** PASS (1,331/1,331)\\n**Overall Verdict:** issues-found (0 critical, 8 warnings, 2 info)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Executive Summary\\n\\nThe romantic-fantasy-writer agent system is architecturally sound after the cycle-1 gap remediation. The critical INV-030 fix is confirmed across all 67 agents. Pipeline hierarchy, convergence bounds, and coordinator purity are all well-implemented. However, this audit found **5 new architectural issues** that the structural checklist cannot detect — these require semantic understanding of the system's behavioral contracts. Additionally, **3 issues from cycle 0** persist (2 at info level, 1 at warning level).\\n\\nNo findings are critical. The system would function correctly at runtime. The warnings affect documentation accuracy, adversarial enforcement depth, and retry mechanism clarity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Perspective 1: Agent-as-Function Compliance\\n\\n### Checks Passed (10/15)\\n\\n| Check | Status | Notes |\\n|-------|--------|-------|\\n| AF-CONTRACT-01 | ✅ PASS | Universal status schema consistent across all agents |\\n| AF-CONTRACT-03 | ✅ PASS | Artifact paths in agent prompts match roster declarations |\\n| AF-CONTRACT-04 | ✅ PASS | N/A — system uses routing tables, not next_hint chains |\\n| AF-ROUTE-01 | ✅ PASS | All coordinator routing tables cover all child result codes |\\n| AF-ROUTE-02 | ✅ PASS | Every routing chain terminates at orchestrator (delivered/failed) |\\n| AF-ROUTE-03 | ✅ PASS | All routing references point to agents in roster (67/67 confirmed) |\\n| AF-ROUTE-04 | ✅ PASS | Parent routing tables cover all children's declared result codes |\\n| AF-MANIFEST-02 | ✅ PASS | Consistent field names across all agents referencing manifests |\\n| AF-MANIFEST-03 | ✅ PASS | Prepend-entry (newest first) consistently specified |\\n| AF-ARTIFACT-03 | ✅ PASS | No agent writes artifacts outside its declared writes list |\\n\\n### Checks Failed (5/15)\\n\\n#### AF-CONTRACT-02 — Skill File Result Code Table Inaccuracy ⚠️ WARNING\\n\\nThe `skills/agent-as-function-contract/SKILL.md` result code table contains two attribution errors:\\n\\n1. **`revision-required`** is listed as a result code used by \\\"Beta-reading-coordinator\\\" — but `revision-required` is NOT a status.json result code. It is a data value inside `beta-synthesis/{N}.json` artifacts. The orchestrator checks for it when reading beta-synthesis data, not as a coordinator result. The beta-reading-coordinator's actual result codes are: `complete`, `blocked`, `revision-loop`.\\n\\n2. **`revision-loop`** is listed as used by \\\"Orchestrator\\\" — but `revision-loop` is emitted by coordinators, not the orchestrator. The orchestrator's result codes are: `delivered`, `delivered-with-gaps`, `failed`. The orchestrator ROUTES on `revision-loop` (it reads it from coordinator status) but does not produce it.\\n\\n**Impact:** An implementing agent reading the skill file would have incorrect expectations about which agents produce which result codes. This could cause routing confusion during integration.\\n\\n**Fix:** Correct the table: remove `revision-required` (it's data, not a result code) or relabel it as \\\"beta-synthesis data field\\\". Change `revision-loop` attribution from \\\"Orchestrator\\\" to \\\"All coordinators (8)\\\".\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### AF-MANIFEST-01 — Pure Routers Lack Manifest Prepend ℹ️ INFO\\n\\n21 agents don't reference manifest.json prepend: all 8 coordinators, all 10 sub-coordinators, the orchestrator, and the guide. The manifest schema says \\\"Every agent prepends an entry after completing work\\\" — but pure routers produce no creative artifacts, only status.json routing signals.\\n\\n**Impact:** Minimal. Pipeline execution is still traceable through status.json files. The manifest would be more complete with router entries, but the omission is defensible.\\n\\n**Carried over from cycle 0.** No change in assessment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### AF-ARTIFACT-01 — Phantom Agent Reference in Schema ⚠️ WARNING\\n\\n`schemas/craft-profile.schema.md` references `romantic-fantasy-writer-plot-architect` as a writer:\\n\\n> *\\\"read-modify-write — Initially created by romantic-fantasy-writer-concept-developer, may be augmented during plotting by romantic-fantasy-writer-plot-architect.\\\"*\\n\\nThis agent **does not exist**. The roster has no `plot-architect`. The only writer of `craft-profile.json` per the roster is `craft-profile-selector`. No agent file named `*plot-architect*` exists in the agents directory.\\n\\n**Impact:** A schema consumer (e.g., an implementing LLM) could expect a plot-architect agent to exist and fail when it doesn't. The schema is the source of truth for artifact contracts — phantom references undermine trust.\\n\\n**Fix:** Remove the plot-architect reference. If the design intent is for plotting agents to augment the craft profile, add the appropriate specialist's name and update the roster.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### AF-ARTIFACT-02 — Schema/Implementation Protocol Mismatch for draft.md ⚠️ WARNING\\n\\n`schemas/chapters.schema.md` declares `chapters/{N}/draft.md` as:\\n\\n> *\\\"**create-once** — Written by romantic-fantasy-writer-chapter-drafter.\\\"*\\n\\nBut the roster declares **4 writers**: chapter-drafter, pov-voice-maintainer, continuity-integrator, craft-enforcer. The three editing agents explicitly describe modifying the draft:\\n\\n- pov-voice-maintainer: *\\\"Edit chapters/{N}/draft.md to correct voice inconsistencies\\\"*\\n- continuity-integrator: *\\\"edit chapters/{N}/draft.md to resolve it\\\"*\\n- craft-enforcer: writes to draft.md per roster\\n\\nThe actual protocol is sequential read-modify-write: drafter creates → voice maintainer edits → continuity integrator edits → craft enforcer edits. The schema says create-once, the implementation says read-modify-write.\\n\\n**Impact:** The `Rules` skill defines create-once as: *\\\"Check that the file does NOT exist before writing. If it exists, you are in a retry.\\\"* If pov-voice-maintainer follows this literally, it would interpret the existing draft as a retry signal rather than the expected sequential editing workflow.\\n\\n**Fix:** Change the schema write protocol to: *\\\"Initial creation by chapter-drafter (create-once). Sequential read-modify-write by: (1) pov-voice-maintainer, (2) continuity-integrator, (3) craft-enforcer. Each editor preserves prior content and makes targeted modifications only.\\\"*\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### AF-ARTIFACT-04 — Create-Once Protocol Violated ⚠️ WARNING\\n\\nDirect consequence of AF-ARTIFACT-02. Three agents (pov-voice-maintainer, continuity-integrator, craft-enforcer) modify a create-once artifact, violating the declared write protocol. The `Rules` skill's create-once guard would cause these agents to behave incorrectly on first invocation (interpreting the existing draft as a retry rather than a normal sequential edit).\\n\\n**Fix:** Resolve jointly with AF-ARTIFACT-02 by updating the schema. Also consider adding a `multi-pass-edit` protocol variant to the Rules skill that distinguishes between retry (auditor sent you back) and sequential editing (you're the next editor in the pipeline).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Perspective 2: Fractal Workflow Evaluation\\n\\n### Checks Passed (14/19)\\n\\n| Check | Status | Notes |\\n|-------|--------|-------|\\n| FW-PIPELINE-01 | ✅ PASS | Pipeline passes execute in strict order (concept→...→polish) |\\n| FW-PIPELINE-02 | ✅ PASS | Each pass has clear entry/exit conditions via progress.json |\\n| FW-PIPELINE-03 | ✅ PASS | Re-entry rules bounded (maxAuditorRetries=3, maxRevisionBetaCycles=2) |\\n| FW-PIPELINE-04 | ✅ PASS | Artifact data flow matches pipeline ordering |\\n| FW-HIERARCHY-02 | ✅ PASS | All coordinators dispatch only their declared children |\\n| FW-HIERARCHY-03 | ✅ PASS | No specialist dispatches other agents (0 instances found) |\\n| FW-HIERARCHY-04 | ✅ PASS | Depth-3 hierarchies correct (worldbuilding, character, plotting, drafting, beta-reading) |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-01 | ✅ PASS | maxAuditorRetries=3, maxRevisionBetaCycles=2 bound all loops |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-02 | ✅ PASS | Retry resets only necessary statuses (not all) |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-03 | ✅ PASS | Convergence detectable via auditor passed + all chapters complete |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-04 | ✅ PASS | Forced delivery: blocked → failed or delivered-with-gaps |\\n| FW-PURITY-01 | ✅ PASS | All 18 coordinators/sub-coordinators have Pure Router Purity Rule |\\n| FW-PURITY-02 | ✅ PASS | No coordinator has creative Process steps (only routing tables) |\\n| FW-LAZY-03 | ✅ PASS | Most auditors include evidence requirements in anti-laziness |\\n\\n### Checks Failed (5/19)\\n\\n#### FW-HIERARCHY-01 — Orchestrator Dispatches 3 Specialists Directly ℹ️ INFO\\n\\nThe orchestrator dispatches 3 specialist-level agents directly:\\n- `craft-tracker` — dispatched between style and drafting phases (initialization)\\n- `continuity-tracker` — dispatched between drafting and revision phases (full verification)\\n- `series-kb-manager` — dispatched after polish (series knowledge base update)\\n\\nThese are cross-cutting concerns that operate at pipeline transition boundaries. Wrapping each in a single-agent coordinator would add routing complexity without architectural benefit.\\n\\n**Carried over from cycle 0.** No change in assessment. Recommended to document as intentional deviation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### FW-PURITY-03 — revision-loop Is a Dead Routing Path ⚠️ WARNING\\n\\n`revision-loop` is declared as a result code for all 8 coordinators. The orchestrator has a routing rule for it:\\n\\n> *\\\"agents/{any-coordinator}/status.json | result == 'revision-loop' | Wait — coordinator is in its internal auditor retry loop.\\\"*\\n\\nBut **no coordinator routing table ever emits revision-loop**. All 8 coordinators have routing rules that produce only `complete` or `blocked`. The `fractal-coordinator-patterns` skill describes the retry loop but never specifies when to write `revision-loop` as a status result.\\n\\nSearched all 8 coordinator prompts for any instruction to write revision-loop: **0 matches**. This means:\\n- The orchestrator routing rule `result == 'revision-loop'` can never be triggered\\n- The result code exists in the roster but is never produced\\n- This is a dead code path in the routing DAG\\n\\n**Impact:** The routing dead path doesn't cause errors (it simply never matches), but it indicates an incomplete design intent. The original purpose was to give the orchestrator a heartbeat signal during coordinator retry loops — this was never implemented in coordinator prompts.\\n\\n**Fix:** Either: (a) Add an explicit step to each coordinator: \\\"Before starting auditor retry, write own status as 'revision-loop'\\\" with a corresponding routing table row, or (b) remove revision-loop from all coordinator result codes and the orchestrator routing table.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### FW-LAZY-01 — Beta Path Adversarial Agents Lack Anti-Laziness ⚠️ WARNING\\n\\n6 adversarial agents in the beta-reading path have **no Anti-Laziness Rules section**:\\n- `beta-synthesizer` — aggregates findings, assigns severity, produces revision verdicts\\n- `romance-beta-reader` — romance lens reviewer\\n- `fantasy-beta-reader` — fantasy lens reviewer\\n- `craft-beta-reader` — craft lens reviewer\\n- `sensitivity-beta-reader` — sensitivity lens reviewer\\n- `originality-beta-reader` — originality lens reviewer\\n\\nAll 8 phase auditors DO have anti-laziness rules. But the beta-reading path's content-producing adversarial agents do not.\\n\\n**Why this matters:** The beta-synthesizer produces `beta-synthesis/{N}.json` with severity ratings that drive the revision-required decision at the orchestrator level. Without anti-laziness rules:\\n- It could under-rate severity to avoid triggering costly revision cycles\\n- It could skip conflict reconciliation between lenses\\n- It could superficially aggregate without thorough de-duplication\\n\\nThe beta readers are the system's last adversarial checkpoint before polish. Without anti-laziness:\\n- They could produce shallow \\\"looks good\\\" feedback\\n- They could avoid finding critical issues to minimize revision work\\n\\n**Carried over from cycle 0.** This was identified in the first audit but not remediated.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### FW-LAZY-04 — Auditor Zero-Findings Suspicion Gaps ⚠️ WARNING\\n\\n4 of 8 phase auditors lack explicit zero-findings suspicion clauses:\\n- `character-auditor` — 5 anti-laziness items, none mention zero-findings\\n- `plotting-auditor` — 5 anti-laziness items, none mention zero-findings\\n- `style-auditor` — 5 anti-laziness items, none mention zero-findings\\n- `revision-auditor` — has evidence requirement but no zero-findings clause\\n\\nCompare with auditors that DO have it:\\n- `worldbuilding-auditor`: *\\\"If your first pass finds zero issues, that is suspicious — do a second pass\\\"*\\n- `concept-auditor`: includes zero-findings suspicion\\n- `drafting-auditor`: includes zero-findings suspicion\\n- `beta-reading-auditor`: strong zero-findings handling (3 references)\\n\\n**Impact:** An auditor without a zero-findings suspicion clause might pass work on first read without a second verification pass. For complex multi-artifact phases (character: 5+ character files, plotting: outlines + tension map + arc timeline, style: per-character voice specs + quality floor), zero findings is statistically improbable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### FW-ADDITIONAL-01 — Specialist Retry Feedback Gap ⚠️ WARNING\\n\\nThe retry feedback mechanism is architecturally fragile. The system relies on a chain of indirection:\\n\\n1. **Auditor** writes `audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json` with specific failure feedback\\n2. **Coordinator** deletes specialist statuses and re-dispatches\\n3. **Specialist** must somehow discover it's in a retry and read the gate file\\n\\nThe chain breaks at step 3. Specialist prompts:\\n- Don't list `audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json` in their Reads\\n- Have no \\\"Step 0: Check for Retry\\\" in their process\\n- Have no instructions for incorporating auditor feedback\\n\\nThe system relies on two skills for this:\\n- `Rules skill`: *\\\"create-once → If it exists, you are in a retry — read the auditor feedback and write an improved version\\\"*\\n- `coordinator-patterns skill`: *\\\"Feedback artifact path: audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json — writers MUST read this on retry\\\"*\\n\\nBut the specialist prompts themselves are self-contained linear processes with no retry awareness. The concept-developer reads ONLY `story-config.json`. The geography-builder has zero retry-related content.\\n\\n**Inconsistency:** Some coordinators DO read gate files during retry (drafting-coordinator: *\\\"read audit-reports/drafting/gate.json\\\"*), while others don't (concept-coordinator's retry rule just deletes statuses and re-dispatches without any gate file reference).\\n\\n**Impact:** On retry, specialists may produce identical output because they don't know what the auditor rejected. The retry loop would consume all maxAuditorRetries without improvement, ending in a `blocked` state. The system \\\"works\\\" in the sense that it eventually gives up, but it wastes 3 retry cycles without meaningful improvement.\\n\\n**Fix:** Add to every specialist prompt: *\\\"Step 0 — Check for Retry: If your primary output artifact already exists, you are in a retry. Read audit-reports/{your-phase}/gate.json for the auditor's specific feedback. Focus your improvements on the issues flagged.\\\"* Also add `audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json` to each specialist's Reads list in roster.json.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Gap Remediation Verification\\n\\n### Critical Gaps (3/3 Verified Fixed)\\n\\n| Gap ID | Description | Status |\\n|--------|------------|--------|\\n| IE-001 | INV-030 missing from all 67 agents | ✅ **FIXED** — grep confirms 67/67 agents reference INV-030 |\\n| IE-002 | INV-063/T25 coverage gap | ✅ **FIXED** — handled through craft-profile dynamic mechanism |\\n| General | Agent file existence and roster alignment | ✅ **FIXED** — 67/67 files exist, 0 missing, 0 extra |\\n\\n### Warning Gaps (10/19 Spot-Checked, All Fixed)\\n\\nThe structural fixes are confirmed. Coordinator purity rules present in all 18 coordinators/sub-coordinators. Skills consistently referenced (agent-as-function-contract + rules in all 67 agents; fractal-coordinator-patterns in all 18 coordinators/sub-coordinators; fractal-orchestrator-architecture in orchestrator).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Architecture Health Assessment\\n\\n**Overall: Good (B+)**\\n\\n**Strengths:**\\n- Clean pipeline hierarchy with well-defined phase boundaries\\n- Comprehensive invariant coverage (81 invariants distributed across relevant agents)\\n- Effective convergence bounds (maxAuditorRetries=3, maxRevisionBetaCycles=2, maxChaptersBeforeCheckpoint=5)\\n- Universal INV-030 coverage after remediation\\n- Clean artifact data flow (27 data flow paths, all traceable)\\n- Strong coordinator purity (all 18 are genuinely pure routers)\\n- Good depth-3 decisions (worldbuilding, character, plotting, drafting, beta-reading)\\n\\n**Weaknesses:**\\n- Beta-reading adversarial layer lacks enforcement teeth (no anti-laziness for 6 agents)\\n- Schema/implementation divergence for draft.md write protocol\\n- Specialist retry mechanism relies on skill indirection rather than explicit prompt instructions\\n- Phantom agent reference in schema documentation\\n- revision-loop dead routing path in orchestrator\\n\\n**Risk Assessment:**\\n- None of the findings threaten fundamental correctness\\n- The retry feedback gap is the most impactful warning — it could cause wasted retry cycles\\n- The beta reader anti-laziness gap is the most impactful for output quality — it leaves the adversarial review layer without enforcement teeth\\n- All issues are fixable without architectural changes — they require prompt/schema updates only\\n\"}"
