eld names across all agents referencing manifests |\\n| AF-MANIFEST-03 | PASS | Prepend-entry (newest first) consistently specified |\\n| AF-ARTIFACT-03 | PASS | No agent writes artifacts outside its declared writes list |\\n\\n### Checks Failed (5/15)\\n\\n#### AF-CONTRACT-02 — Skill File Result Code Table Inaccuracy (WARNING)\\n\\nThe `skills/agent-as-function-contract/SKILL.md` result code table contains two attribution errors:\\n\\n1. **`revision-required`** is listed as a result code used by \\\"Beta-reading-coordinator\\\" — but revision-required is NOT a status.json result code. It is a data value inside `beta-synthesis/{N}.json` artifacts. The orchestrator checks for it when reading beta-synthesis data, not as a coordinator result. The beta-reading-coordinator's actual result codes are: `complete`, `blocked`, `revision-loop`.\\n\\n2. **`revision-loop`** is listed as used by \\\"Orchestrator\\\" — but `revision-loop` is emitted by coordinators, not the orchestrator. The orchestrator's result codes are: `delivered`, `delivered-with-gaps`, `failed`. The orchestrator ROUTES on `revision-loop` (it reads it from coordinator status) but does not produce it.\\n\\n**Impact:** An implementing agent reading the skill file would have incorrect expectations about which agents produce which result codes.\\n\\n**Fix:** Correct the table: remove `revision-required` (it's data, not a result code) or relabel it as \\\"beta-synthesis data field\\\". Change `revision-loop` attribution from \\\"Orchestrator\\\" to \\\"All coordinators (8)\\\".\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### AF-MANIFEST-01 — Pure Routers Lack Manifest Prepend (INFO)\\n\\n21 agents don't reference manifest.json prepend: all 8 coordinators, all 10 sub-coordinators, the orchestrator, and the guide. The manifest schema says \\\"Every agent prepends an entry after completing work\\\" — but pure routers produce no creative artifacts, only status.json routing signals.\\n\\n**Carried over from cycle 0.** No change in assessment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### AF-ARTIFACT-01 — Phantom Agent Reference in Schema (WARNING)\\n\\n`schemas/craft-profile.schema.md` references `romantic-fantasy-writer-plot-architect` as a writer:\\n\\n> *\\\"read-modify-write — Initially created by romantic-fantasy-writer-concept-developer, may be augmented during plotting by romantic-fantasy-writer-plot-architect.\\\"*\\n\\nThis agent **does not exist**. The roster has no `plot-architect`. Only `craft-profile-selector` writes `craft-profile.json`.\\n\\n**Fix:** Remove the plot-architect reference from the schema.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### AF-ARTIFACT-02 — Schema/Implementation Protocol Mismatch for draft.md (WARNING)\\n\\n`schemas/chapters.schema.md` declares `chapters/{N}/draft.md` as `create-once` written only by chapter-drafter. But the roster declares 4 writers and 3 agent prompts explicitly describe editing the draft:\\n- pov-voice-maintainer: *\\\"Edit chapters/{N}/draft.md to correct voice inconsistencies\\\"*\\n- continuity-integrator: *\\\"edit chapters/{N}/draft.md to resolve it\\\"*\\n- craft-enforcer: writes to draft.md per roster\\n\\nThe actual protocol is sequential read-modify-write. The schema says create-once.\\n\\n**Impact:** The Rules skill defines create-once as: *\\\"Check that the file does NOT exist before writing. If it exists, you are in a retry.\\\"* Editors following this literally would misinterpret the existing draft as a retry signal.\\n\\n**Fix:** Change schema to read-modify-write with sequential ordering specification.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### AF-ARTIFACT-04 — Create-Once Protocol Violated (WARNING)\\n\\nDirect consequence of AF-ARTIFACT-02. Three agents modify a create-once artifact, violating the declared write protocol.\\n\\n**Fix:** Resolve jointly with AF-ARTIFACT-02. Consider adding a `multi-pass-edit` protocol variant to the Rules skill.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Perspective 2: Fractal Workflow Evaluation\\n\\n### Checks Passed (14/19)\\n\\n| Check | Status | Notes |\\n|-------|--------|-------|\\n| FW-PIPELINE-01 | PASS | Pipeline passes execute in strict order |\\n| FW-PIPELINE-02 | PASS | Clear entry/exit conditions via progress.json |\\n| FW-PIPELINE-03 | PASS | Re-entry bounded (maxAuditorRetries=3, maxRevisionBetaCycles=2) |\\n| FW-PIPELINE-04 | PASS | Artifact data flow matches pipeline ordering |\\n| FW-HIERARCHY-02 | PASS | Coordinators dispatch only declared children |\\n| FW-HIERARCHY-03 | PASS | No specialist dispatches other agents |\\n| FW-HIERARCHY-04 | PASS | Depth-3 hierarchies correct |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-01 | PASS | All loops bounded |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-02 | PASS | Selective status reset on retry |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-03 | PASS | Convergence detectable |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-04 | PASS | Forced delivery path exists |\\n| FW-PURITY-01 | PASS | All 18 coordinators/sub-coordinators have Purity Rule |\\n| FW-PURITY-02 | PASS | No coordinator has creative Process steps |\\n| FW-LAZY-03 | PASS | Most auditors include evidence requirements |\\n\\n### Checks Failed (5/19)\\n\\n#### FW-HIERARCHY-01 — Orchestrator Dispatches 3 Specialists (INFO)\\n\\nThe orchestrator dispatches craft-tracker, continuity-tracker, and series-kb-manager directly. These are cross-cutting concerns at pipeline boundaries. Architecturally justified.\\n\\n**Carried over from cycle 0.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### FW-PURITY-03 — revision-loop Is a Dead Routing Path (WARNING)\\n\\n`revision-loop` is declared for all 8 coordinators but **no coordinator routing table ever emits it**. The orchestrator has a routing rule for it that can never be triggered. This is a dead code path in the routing DAG.\\n\\n**Fix:** Either add explicit emission instructions to coordinator prompts, or remove the dead path entirely.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### FW-LAZY-01 — Beta Path Adversarial Agents Lack Anti-Laziness (WARNING)\\n\\n6 adversarial agents have no Anti-Laziness Rules: beta-synthesizer and all 5 beta readers. The synthesizer controls revision decisions; beta readers are the adversarial review layer.\\n\\n**Carried over from cycle 0