              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/agents/fractal-factory-audit-oracle/output.md\", \"file_text\": \"# Audit Oracle — Iteration 3 (Gap-Cycle 2 Re-Verification)\\n\\n**Agent:** fractal-factory-audit-oracle\\n**Iteration:** 3\\n**Context:** Re-verification after gap-hunting cycle 2 fixed 6 warning gaps\\n**Checklist-Validator Status:** PASS (1,326/1,326)\\n**Cycle 1 Oracle:** 0 critical, 8 warnings, 2 info\\n**Overall Verdict:** issues-found (0 critical, 6 warnings, 2 info)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Executive Summary\\n\\nThe romantic-fantasy-writer agent system continues to improve. All 6 cycle-2 gap fixes have been independently verified — the craft-profile schema (AC-004), draft.md protocol (AC-005), ghost agent removal (CR-002), result code table correction (CR-003), README counts (DC-W05), and external reference cleanup (MK-W03) are all confirmed resolved from an independent architectural perspective.\\n\\nOf the 8 warnings from cycle 1, **2 are fully resolved** (AF-CONTRACT-02, AF-ARTIFACT-01), **3 are partially resolved** (AF-ARTIFACT-02/04 fixed for draft.md but revealed 3 similar artifacts; FW-LAZY-04 improved — all auditors now have sections but 4 still incomplete), and **3 are unresolved** (FW-PURITY-03, FW-LAZY-01, FW-ADDITIONAL-01). The 2 info findings remain as architectural design decisions.\\n\\nThe **new finding** this cycle is an expansion of AF-ARTIFACT-02: three additional artifact families (`characters/index.json`, `characters/{CHAR-NNN}.json`, `chapter-outlines/{N}.json`) have the same create-once/read-modify-write mismatch that was fixed for `draft.md`. This is a systematic pattern, not an isolated bug.\\n\\nNo findings are critical. The system would function correctly at runtime. The warnings affect write-protocol contract accuracy, adversarial enforcement depth, retry mechanism clarity, and routing DAG hygiene.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cycle 2 Fix Verification (All 6 Independently Confirmed)\\n\\n### AC-004: craft-profile.schema.md Protocol — ✅ VERIFIED FIXED\\n\\n**What was wrong:** Schema declared `read-modify-write` with two writers (concept-developer + plot-architect), but architecture.json declared `create-once` with a single writer.\\n\\n**What I verified:** `craft-profile.schema.md` now correctly reads:\\n> *\\\"**create-once** — Created by `romantic-fantasy-writer-craft-profile-selector` during the concept phase. Once written, craft-profile.json is immutable — selected tools become binding invariants (INV-069, INV-079).\\\"*\\n\\nWriters section lists only `romantic-fantasy-writer-craft-profile-selector (sole writer — concept phase)`. Architecture.json now consistent. **Fix is clean.**\\n\\n### AC-005: chapters/{N}/draft.md Protocol — ✅ VERIFIED FIXED\\n\\n**What was wrong:** Schema declared `create-once` with 1 writer, but roster declared 4 writers.\\n\\n**What I verified:** `chapters.schema.md` now declares:\\n> *\\\"**read-modify-write** — Sequential editing pipeline with 4 writers:\\\"*\\n\\nLists all 4 writers with execution ordering, includes `draftVersion` frontmatter incrementing (1→2→3→4), and documents the reset-on-retry behavior. This is exactly the fix needed. **Fix is clean.**\\n\\n### CR-002: Ghost Agent 'plot-architect' — ✅ VERIFIED FIXED\\n\\n**What was wrong:** `craft-profile.schema.md` referenced `romantic-fantasy-writer-plot-architect`, a non-existent agent.\\n\\n**What I verified:** `grep -r plot-architect agents/ schemas/ skills/` returns zero matches across all produced files. The ghost agent is completely purged. **Fix is clean.**\\n\\n### CR-003: Skill Result Code Table — ✅ VERIFIED FIXED\\n\\n**What was wrong:** `agent-as-function-contract/SKILL.md` misattributed `revision-required` and `revision-loop`.\\n\\n**What I verified:** The table now correctly states:\\n- `revision-required`: \\\"beta-synthesizer (data field in synthesis output, surfaced by beta-reading-coordinator)\\\"\\n- `revision-loop`: \\\"All phase coordinators (8) — orchestrator consumes this code for cross-phase routing\\\"\\n\\n**Note:** The `revision-loop` attribution is now factually correct about *declaration* but architecturally misleading — see FW-PURITY-03 below (no coordinator can actually produce this code). The table is correct for what the roster says; the roster is what's wrong. **Fix is clean for what was asked.**\\n\\n### DC-W05: README Priority Counts — ✅ VERIFIED FIXED\\n\\n**What was wrong:** README.md claimed P0=6, P1=14, P2=6 but actual test scenarios had different counts.\\n\\n**What I verified:** README now states P0=7, P1=16, P2=3 (total: 26). The P0 scenarios table lists 7 tests: TEST-001, TEST-002, TEST-003, TEST-005, TEST-020, TEST-021, TEST-022. All 26 test directories (TEST-001 through TEST-026) exist with context.json, expected-status.json, and README.md files. **Fix is clean.**\\n\\n### MK-W03: External 'Ralph Orchestrator' Reference — ✅ VERIFIED FIXED\\n\\n**What was wrong:** `mcp-sidecar/SKILL.md` contained an external reference to 'Ralph Orchestrator'.\\n\\n**What I verified:** `grep -r 'Ralph' skills/` and `grep -r 'ralph' skills/` both return zero matches. The mcp-sidecar/SKILL.md file has 109 lines with no external references. All 9 skills are now clean of external references. **Fix is clean.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Perspective 1: Agent-as-Function Compliance\\n\\n### Checks Passed (13/16)\\n\\n| Check | Status | Notes |\\n|-------|--------|-------|\\n| AF-CONTRACT-01 | PASS | Universal status schema consistent across all 67 agents |\\n| AF-CONTRACT-02 | PASS | **Resolved in cycle 2** — Result code table now accurate |\\n| AF-CONTRACT-03 | PASS | Artifact paths in agent prompts match roster declarations |\\n| AF-CONTRACT-04 | PASS | N/A — system uses routing tables, not next_hint chains |\\n| AF-ROUTE-01 | PASS | All coordinator routing tables cover all child result codes |\\n| AF-ROUTE-02 | PASS | Every routing chain terminates at orchestrator (delivered/failed) |\\n| AF-ROUTE-03 | PASS | All routing references point to agents in roster (67/67 confirmed via exhaustive regex extraction of `romantic-fantasy-writer-*` names from routing actions) |\\n| AF-ROUTE-04 | PASS | Parent routing tables cover all children's declared result codes |\\n| AF-MANIFEST-02 | PASS | Consistent field names across all agents referencing manifests |\\n| AF-MANIFEST-03 | PASS | Prepend-entry (newest first) consistently specified |\\n| AF-ARTIFACT-01 | PASS | **Resolved in cycle 2** — Ghost agent reference removed |\\n| AF-ARTIFACT-03 | PASS | No agent writes artifacts outside its declared writes list |\\n| AF-ROUTE-01 (extended) | PASS | Routing tables in routing.json verified: all 20 coordinator/sub-coordinator entries present, dispatch orders reference only roster agents |\\n\\n### Checks Failed (3/16)\\n\\n#### AF-ARTIFACT-02 — Create-Once Protocol Mismatch for 3 Artifact Families (WARNING)\\n\\nThe `draft.md` fix (AC-005) correctly resolved one instance of this pattern. But the same systematic issue exists in three more artifact families. All three are labeled `create-once` in both `architecture.json` and their schema files, but their agent prompts describe read-modify-write operations:\\n\\n**1. `characters/index.json`** — architecture.json: create-once, 2 writers\\n- `protagonist-profiler` creates it first with lead characters\\n- `supporting-cast-developer` prompt L48: *\\\"Update characters/index.json with new entries\\\"* — this is an explicit modify operation on an existing file\\n- Schema says `create-once — Written by protagonist-profiler and supporting-cast-developer`\\n- **Conflict:** The Rules skill defines create-once as *\\\"Check that the file does NOT exist before writing. If it exists, you are in a retry.\\\"* Supporting-cast-developer finding an existing index.json would interpret it as a retry condition.\\n\\n**2. `characters/{CHAR-NNN}.json`** — architecture.json: create-once, 3 writers\\n- `protagonist-profiler` creates lead character files (e.g., CHAR-001)\\n- `supporting-cast-developer` creates supporting character files (e.g., CHAR-003) — **these are different files**, so create-once per individual file is technically valid for these two agents\\n- `character-voice-designer` prompt L48: *\\\"Write updated characters/CHAR-NNN.json files with refined voiceFingerprint and sensorySig fields\\\"* — explicitly editing existing files\\n- **Conflict:** The voice-designer edits files created by other agents. This is read-modify-write, not create-once.\\n\\n**3. `chapter-outlines/{N}.json`** — architecture.json: create-once, 2 writers\\n- `chapter-outliner` creates the outline for chapter N (step 7: \\\"Write chapter-outlines/{N}.json\\\")\\n- `scene-beat-designer` prompt step 6: *\\\"Update chapter-outlines/{N}.json\\\"* — enriching the same files with granular scene beats\\n- Schema says `create-once — Written by chapter-outliner and scene-beat-designer`\\n- **Conflict:** scene-beat-designer modifies existing files, violating create-once semantics.\\n\\n**Impact:** Medium. At runtime, agents following Rules skill strictly would misinterpret existing files as retry signals. The coordinator-patterns skill describes retry via status deletion, which partially mitigates this, but the write protocol labels create confusing contracts.\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Apply the same fix pattern used for draft.md (AC-005):\\n1. Change architecture.json `writeProtocol` to `read-modify-write` for all three\\n2. Update schema files with sequential writer ordering and version tracking\\n3. For characters/{CHAR-NNN}.json, consider split: create-once for profiler/cast-developer (different files), read-modify-write for voice-designer (editing existing files)\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### AF-ARTIFACT-04 — Create-Once Violations (WARNING)\\n\\nDirect consequence of AF-ARTIFACT-02. Three agents (supporting-cast-developer, character-voice-designer, scene-beat-designer) perform modify operations on artifacts declared as create-once.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### AF-MANIFEST-01 — Pure Routers Lack Manifest Prepend (INFO)\\n\\n21 agents don't reference manifest.json prepend: 1 orchestrator, 8 coordinators, 10 sub-coordinators, 1 guide, and the orchestrator's direct specialist dispatches. This is architecturally correct — manifest tracks creative output, not routing decisions. Carried from cycle 0.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Perspective 2: Fractal Workflow Evaluation\\n\\n### Checks Passed (11/16)\\n\\n| Check | Status | Notes |\\n|-------|--------|-------|\\n| FW-PIPELINE-01 | PASS | Pipeline passes in strict sequential order. No pass references a later pass. |\\n| FW-PIPELINE-02 | PASS | Clear entry/exit conditions in architecture.json for all 8 passes |\\n| FW-PIPELINE-03 | PASS | Re-entry bounded: maxAuditorRetries=3 (coordinator-patterns skill L26), maxRevisionBetaCycles defined in orchestrator routing |\\n| FW-PIPELINE-04 | PASS | Artifact data flow matches pipeline ordering (38 domain + 4 universal artifacts traced) |\\n| FW-HIERARCHY-02 | PASS | All coordinators dispatch only their declared children (verified via routing.json dispatch orders against roster children) |\\n| FW-HIERARCHY-03 | PASS | No specialist dispatches other agents (grep for 'dispatch romantic-fantasy-writer' in specialist prompts: zero matches) |\\n| FW-HIERARCHY-04 | PASS | Depth-3 hierarchies correct: worldbuilding, character, plotting, drafting, beta-reading all use coordinator→sub-coordinator→specialist chain |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-01 | PASS | All loops bounded: maxAuditorRetries=3 (skill), maxRevisionBetaCycles (orchestrator routing), maxChaptersBeforeCheckpoint=5 |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-02 | PASS | Selective status reset on retry (cascade delete of child statuses, not all pipeline state) |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-03 | PASS | Convergence detectable: coordinator reports complete/blocked; orchestrator checks beta-synthesis verdicts |\\n| FW-CONVERGE-04 | PASS | Forced delivery: polish-coordinator blocked → delivered-with-gaps; series-kb blocked → delivered-with-gaps |\\n\\n### Checks Failed (5/16)\\n\\n#### FW-PURITY-03 — revision-loop Is a Dead Routing Path (WARNING)\\n\\n**Persists from cycle 1. Not addressed by any gap.**\\n\\n`revision-loop` is declared as a result code for all 8 phase coordinators (concept, worldbuilding, character, plotting, style, drafting, revision, beta-reading) and all 10 sub-coordinators in roster.json. However, **no coordinator routing table contains a code path that produces `revision-loop`**.\\n\\nExhaustive analysis of all 19 coordinator routing tables in routing.json confirms:\\n- Every coordinator's achievable output set is exactly `{complete, blocked}`\\n- No routing rule action contains `write own status: revision-loop` or any variant\\n- The only coordinator with 2 result codes (no revision-loop) is polish-coordinator: `{complete, blocked}`\\n\\nThe orchestrator's routing table row:\\n> `agents/{any-coordinator}/status.json | result == 'revision-loop' | Wait — coordinator is in its internal auditor retry loop. No action needed; coordinator will eventually resolve to 'complete' or 'blocked'. Re-read on next routing cycle.`\\n\\nThis rule can **never trigger** because no coordinator can produce `revision-loop`. It's dead code in the routing DAG.\\n\\n**Semantic analysis:** The `revision-loop` code was likely intended to represent the coordinator's intermediate state during auditor retries. But coordinators re-dispatch children internally and only write their own status when the loop terminates (either `complete` after passing or `blocked` after max retries). They don't write intermediate status.\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Option A (recommended): Add explicit emission. When a coordinator fails its auditor gate and begins a retry (retries > 0, retries < max), have it write `status.json` with `result: 'revision-loop'` *before* deleting child statuses and re-dispatching. This gives the orchestrator visibility into retry state. Option B: Remove `revision-loop` from all coordinator result codes and the orchestrator routing table.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### FW-HIERARCHY-01 — Orchestrator Dispatches 3 Specialists (INFO)\\n\\n**Carried from cycle 0. Design decision.**\\n\\nThe orchestrator dispatches `continuity-tracker`, `series-kb-manager`, and `craft-tracker` directly. These are cross-cutting pipeline boundary agents operating between phases, not within any coordinator's creative phase.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### FW-LAZY-01 — Beta Path Adversarial Agents Lack Anti-Laziness (WARNING)\\n\\n**Persists from cycle 1. Not addressed by any gap.**\\n\\nSix adversarial beta-path agents completely lack Anti-Laziness sections:\\n1. `romantic-fantasy-writer-beta-synthesizer` — Controls revision/approve decision\\n2. `romantic-fantasy-writer-romance-beta-reader` — Romance quality review\\n3. `romantic-fantasy-writer-fantasy-beta-reader` — Fantasy quality review\\n4. `romantic-fantasy-writer-craft-beta-reader` — Craft quality review\\n5. `romantic-fantasy-writer-sensitivity-beta-reader` — Sensitivity review\\n6. `romantic-fantasy-writer-originality-beta-reader` — Originality review\\n\\nAll 8 auditors now have Anti-Laziness sections (improvement from cycle 1), but the beta readers — which perform the same adversarial function in the beta-reading phase — have none. The beta-synthesizer is particularly critical as it makes the binary revision/approve decision.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### FW-LAZY-04 — Auditor Anti-Laziness Section Incompleteness (WARNING)\\n\\n**Partially improved from cycle 1.**\\n\\nAll 8 auditors now have Anti-Laziness sections (up from ~4 in cycle 0). However, sections vary in completeness:\\n\\n| Auditor | Section | Zero-Suspicion | Evidence | Item-by-Item |\\n|---------|---------|----------------|----------|--------------|\\n| concept-auditor | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\\n| drafting-auditor | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | — |\\n| worldbuilding-auditor | ✓ | ✓ | — | — |\\n| beta-reading-auditor | ✓ | ✓ | — | ✓ |\\n| character-auditor | ✓ | **✗** | **✗** | ✓ |\\n| plotting-auditor | ✓ | **✗** | **✗** | ✓ |\\n| revision-auditor | ✓ | **✗** | ✓ | ✓ |\\n| style-auditor | ✓ | **✗** | **✗** | — |\\n\\n4 auditors still lack zero-findings suspicion. 4 lack evidence requirements. 3 lack item-by-item verification. Only concept-auditor has all four components.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### FW-ADDITIONAL-01 — Specialist Retry Feedback Gap (WARNING)\\n\\n**Persists from cycle 1. Not addressed by any gap.**\\n\\nThe coordinator-patterns skill explicitly states (line 28):\\n> *\\\"Feedback artifact path: `audit-reports/{phase}/gate.json` — writers MUST read this on retry\\\"*\\n\\nBut **0 of 37 non-auditor specialist prompts** reference `gate.json` or `audit-reports/` in their Reads section. When a coordinator retries after auditor failure:\\n1. Coordinator deletes child status files\\n2. Coordinator re-dispatches writers\\n3. Writers see the same inputs (story-concept, world-bible, etc.)\\n4. Writers produce effectively identical output\\n5. Auditor re-evaluates → likely same result → infinite retry until max\\n\\nThe skill says writers MUST read the gate file; no writer does. The convergence bound (maxAuditorRetries=3) prevents infinite loops, but the retry mechanism is ineffective — it wastes 3 iterations producing the same output rather than incorporating auditor feedback.\\n\\n**Impact:** Medium. The system won't hang (bounded), but retries are wasted computation. A 3-retry cycle with identical output is a guaranteed convergence failure.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Comparison with Checklist Validator\\n\\nThe checklist-validator passed 1,326/1,326 structural checks. This audit found issues the structural checklist inherently cannot detect:\\n\\n| Finding | Why Checklist Misses It |\\n|---------|----------------------|\\n| AF-ARTIFACT-02 (create-once mismatch, 3 artifacts) | Requires cross-referencing write protocol labels against agent prompt verbs (\\\"Update\\\", \\\"Write updated\\\") — semantic analysis |\\n| FW-PURITY-03 (dead routing path) | Requires tracing routing DAG reachability — the checklist verifies routing entries exist, not whether they're reachable |\\n| FW-LAZY-01 (beta anti-laziness) | Checklist checks auditors have anti-laziness; beta readers aren't classified as auditors |\\n| FW-LAZY-04 (incomplete anti-laziness) | Checklist checks for section existence, not content completeness |\\n| FW-ADDITIONAL-01 (specialist retry gap) | Requires understanding the behavioral contract between skill instructions and agent prompts — cross-document semantic analysis |\\n\\nThis demonstrates the continued value of the dual-perspective architectural audit beyond structural validation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Architecture Health Assessment\\n\\n**Overall: Good (B+)**\\n\\nThe system is architecturally sound. Pipeline hierarchy is correct. Convergence bounds exist (via skill indirection). Coordinator purity is properly enforced. 67 agents, 38 domain artifacts, 9 skills, and 26 test scenarios form a coherent production pipeline.\\n\\n**Trajectory:** Improving. Cycle 2 resolved 2 of 8 warnings cleanly and partially improved 1 more. The remaining issues are in three categories:\\n1. **Write protocol labeling** (AF-ARTIFACT-02/04) — systematic but mechanical fix\\n2. **Adversarial enforcement depth** (FW-LAZY-01/04) — adding sections/clauses\\n3. **Behavioral contract gaps** (FW-PURITY-03, FW-ADDITIONAL-01) — routing semantics and retry mechanism\\n\\nNone threaten fundamental correctness. The system would produce valid romantic fantasy chapters. The warnings affect quality assurance reliability (lazy auditors/beta readers), retry efficiency (wasted iterations), and contract clarity (confusing write protocols).\\n\"}"
